## 5. Insights

- `spr.v5.b32`: 91% of the scalar add peak, 23% of the SIMD peak, 24% of the roofline (geomean). 1/20 points sit left of the ridge (DRAM-bound roof).
  - Far below every ceiling: neither DRAM bandwidth nor the add units are the limit. The time goes into latency: index loads, gathers, short loops with data-dependent trip counts (branch mispredicts).
  - ns/add rises strongly with k in 14/20 cases: larger k saves additions but each one costs more.
- `spr.v6.b32`: 87% of the scalar add peak, 22% of the SIMD peak, 23% of the roofline (geomean). 1/20 points sit left of the ridge (DRAM-bound roof).
  - Far below every ceiling: neither DRAM bandwidth nor the add units are the limit. The time goes into latency: index loads, gathers, short loops with data-dependent trip counts (branch mispredicts).
  - Format re-read traffic reaches 323 GB/s (2048×8192, s=70%), 51% of L2 bandwidth: streaming the format once per row of X is a real cost. Reading it once per block (block-outer loop, M-tile inner) removes it.
  - ns/add rises strongly with k in 11/20 cases: larger k saves additions but each one costs more.

